  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/4647">Corollary <span data-tag="4647">7.2.4.17</span>.</a>Grothendieck's vanishing theorem!nonabelian version Let $X$ be a Heyting space of Heyting dimension $\leq n$, and let $\calF $ be an $n$-connective sheaf on $X$. Then for any compact open $U \subseteq X$, the map $\pi _0 \calF (X) \rightarrow \pi _0 \calF (U)$ is surjective. In particular, $\Shv (X)$ has homotopy dimension $\leq n$. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Suppose first that $(1)$ is satisfied. Let $\calF $ be an $n$-connective sheaf on $X$. Then $\calF $ is strongly $0$-connective; by characterization $(2)$ of Theorem <a href="/tag/DA59" data-tag="DA59">7.2.4.14</a>, we deduce that $\calF (X) \rightarrow \calF (U)$ is surjective. The last claim follows by taking $U = \emptyset $. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>